gm-method (live GPU frame timing with EXT_disjoint_timer_query_webgl2 -- the measurement is easy to get WRONG and the wrong number falsely looks like a regression): the timer must bracket exactly ONE call to the real THREE WebGLRenderer.render -- begin query, call the renderer's own render(scene,cam), end query, all synchronously WITHIN one render, then harvest the result a frame or two LATER. Two failure modes seen in one run, both producing garbage ~13-50ms numbers (which are just vsync-frame-SPACING, not GPU work) that look like a 3x regression off the true ~4ms: (1) bracketing the query across a requestAnimationFrame YIELD (beginQuery, then endQuery inside the NEXT rAF) -- that measures wall-clock frame interval incl idle/vsync wait, not GPU draw time; (2) hooking the WRONG renderer handle (window.renderer is often undefined; the real one is nested, e.g. window.__app.renderer -- discover it by walking the app object for isWebGLRenderer/domElement+getContext+render, never assume window.renderer). Correct method (hook __app.renderer.render, wrap one render in begin/endQuery, harvest via setInterval checking QUERY_RESULT_AVAILABLE && !GPU_DISJOINT_EXT) gave p50 4.2ms of a 16ms vsync budget on real AMD/ANGLE-D3D11 -- in-budget, ~12ms slack, vsync-limited not GPU-bound, so no VS/draw win can raise FPS on that device. ALSO: to decide if a sibling-SDK perf commit at HEAD is already shipped, do NOT trust the sibling package.json version (CI bumps it at publish via [skip ci] AFTER the feature commit, so HEAD's package.json reads one BELOW the published version) -- instead npm pack the published tarball, tr -d CR to normalize CRLF-vs-LF (a tarball is LF, a Windows checkout is CRLF, so a raw diff shows EVERY line changed = pure EOL noise, not content), then md5sum/diff the key source file vs the sibling HEAD; identical md5 = the win is shipped+consumed, no bump needed.